      "id": 1,
      "text": "New Market was originally called Jacksonville, named by Jacob Adamson, a Tennesseean who admired Andrew Jackson.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Market%2C_Missouri",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A post office called New Market opened in 1839 after Adamson learned Jacksonville was taken, and it operated until 1959.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Market%2C_Missouri",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The community sits on Missouri Route 371 about 1.5 mi south of the Platte\u2013Buchanan county line.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Market%2C_Missouri",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "route_only"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Bee Creek winds past the west side of New Market, adding a gentle waterway to the town\u2019s landscape.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
